<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aspar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 400–471</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Flavius Ardabur Aspar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Flavius Ardabur Aspar was an Eastern Roman general of Alan descent who dominated the court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> for much of the mid-fifth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">Consul</a> in 434 and long a senior commander, he helped place the emperors Marcian in 450 and Leo I in 457 on the throne, but as an Arian Christian of barbarian origin could not rule himself. Leo I had him and his son Ardabur murdered in 471.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aspar.jpg" alt="Aspar" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: sailko · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piatto_di_ardaburio,_argento_fuso,_434_d.c._(found_in_1769)_03.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Aspar was the son of the magister Ardaburius and was of Alanic-Gothic descent. His name, in Iranian languages, means horse-rider. He rose as the general of a Germanic army in Roman service and held the rank of patrician and magister militum, master of soldiers. He played a crucial part in his father's expedition of 424 that defeated the Western usurper Joannes of Ravenna and installed Galla Placidia and her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-iii">Valentinian III</a>, and he helped to negotiate a peace treaty with Geiseric after the Vandal invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. He reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a> in 434 after campaigning in Africa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Aspar could not become emperor because of his Arian religion, so he acted as a kingmaker. His subordinate Marcian became emperor by marrying Pulcheria, the sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a>. When Marcian died on 27 January 457 the leading figures of the Eastern court took eleven days to choose a successor. Anthemius, Marcian's son-in-law and a magister militum, was a strong candidate, and Aspar was probably offered the throne by the senate but refused. He could have chosen his own son Ardabur but instead selected Leo I, an obscure tribune of one of his military units.</p><p>In 470, in a struggle for power between Aspar and the Isaurian general Zeno, Aspar persuaded the emperor to appoint his second son, Patricius, as caesar and to give him his daughter Leontia in marriage. The clergy and people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> did not think an Arian could become emperor, and riots broke out in the hippodrome, led by Marcellus, head of the Sleepless Monks. Aspar and Leo promised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> that Patricius would convert to Orthodoxy before he became emperor, and that the marriage would follow only after the conversion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 471 a conspiracy organised by Leo I and the Isaurians caused the death of Aspar and his elder son Ardabur. Patricius may have died on this occasion, though some sources report that he recovered from his wounds. Aspar's wife was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoth</a>, and the Ostrogoth King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great">Theodoric the Great</a> was her nephew. A cistern attributed to Aspar still exists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>. His death ended the Germanic domination of Eastern Roman policy, which he had influenced through the reigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a>, Marcian and Leo I.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aspar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Alan soldiers and commanders rose high in Roman service; the general <b>Aspar</b>, of Alan descent, was one of the most powerful men in the Eastern Roman Empire in the mid-fifth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Dengizich attacked the Romans in 467 without his brother Ernak, was besieged, and was defeated by the general <b>Aspar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Marcian, a domesticus who had served the commanders Ardabur and <b>Aspar</b> for fifteen years, was elected and inaugurated emperor of the East after the death of Theodosius II.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-25">25 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Backed by the powerful general <b>Aspar</b>, Leo I became emperor in Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-7">7 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=446" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5259 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Aspar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5709 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=446">What was here in 446 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
